{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8aaf4faba2ff92e6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013 (FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack)\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | FIPS remediation of KServe and serving data plane images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | Platform Operator FIPS compliance | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | Compliance evidence package and certification submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | CI gating check for FIPS regressions | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | FIPS support posture documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n21\t\n22\t## Dependency DAG\n23\t\n24\t```mermaid\n25\tgraph TD\n26\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 KServe image FIPS remediation] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 Platform Operator FIPS compliance]\n27\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 Evidence package and submission]\n28\t    E002 --> E003\n29\t    E001 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 CI FIPS regression gate]\n30\t    E002 --> E004\n31\t    E001 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 FIPS documentation]\n32\t    E002 --> E005\n33\t    E003 --> E005\n34\t    E004 --> E005\n35\t```\n36\t\n37\t## DAG Justification\n38\t\n39\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n40\t|------|------|-----------|\n41\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | The operator-compliance epic's end-to-end verification deploys and reconciles the serving stack using the FIPS-remediated serving images produced by the image remediation (its AC requires CR Ready for an InferenceService using remediated images), so it cannot complete before those images exist. The operator's own image audit/remediation is independent work, but the epic's verification deliverable consumes the remediated image set. |\n42\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | The evidence package consumes the module inventory, remediation records, and FIPS test results produced by the image remediation; it cannot be assembled before those artifacts exist. |\n43\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | Same: the operator's remediation records and test results are inputs to the evidence package. |\n44\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (builds against remediated state) | The gating check's pass/fail baseline is derived from the remediated image set; enabling the gate before remediation would fail every serving-stack build. |\n45\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (builds against remediated state) | The gate covers all serving-stack builds including the platform operator's image builds, so the operator's baseline must be clean first. |\n46\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n47\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n48\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built, including the certification posture. |\n49\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built, including the regression-enforcement posture. |\n50\t\n51\tE001 and E002 touch different components/repos for their own audit and remediation work, but E002's verification deliverable consumes the remediated serving image set produced by E001, so E002 blocks on E001 (Rule 13).\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n54\t\n55\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epic(s) |\n56\t|-----|----------|------------------------|---------|\n57\t| HLR-1 | P0 | All serving-stack images run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto modules | RHAISTRAT-9013-E001, RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 |\n58\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Compliance evidence package assembled and submitted through the security review board to the external certification lab | RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 |\n59\t| HLR-3 | P1 | CI enforces FIPS regressions with a gating check | RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 |\n60\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 |\n61\t\n62\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n63\t\n64\t## Component Mapping Notes\n65\t\n66\t- **KServe** is a canonical component (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) and has an architecture-context file (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`): Stable (GA), owning team RHOAI Serving, midstream `opendatahub-io/kserve`, Konflux-onboarded. E001, E003, and E004 are assigned component `KServe`.\n67\t- The strategy's phrase \"KServe components and the serving data plane\" is tracked under the single `KServe` component per the strategy's own Affected Components list. The serving data plane may overlap Serving Runtimes images (per the architecture context, KServe depends on Serving Runtimes for model execution); if remediation work lands in Serving Runtimes repos, the mapping should be revisited — flagged as a health warning below.\n68\t- **Platform Operator** is a canonical Jira component but has **no architecture-context file**. It is treated as an existing in-platform component (the strategy presumes a deployed operator managing the serving stack, and the component is in the canonical list) rather than triggering the new-component onboarding chain — the strategy's problem is non-validated crypto in existing images, not component provisioning. Its in-platform status is nonetheless unverified; flagged as a health warning.\n69\t- E004's gate spans all serving-stack build pipelines, including the platform operator's builds; the single most-relevant canonical name `KServe` is assigned and the span noted here.\n70\t- E005 is assigned component `Documentation` (canonical) for the docs-authoring work stream.\n71\t\n72\t## Health Warnings\n73\t\n74\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no per-component change/owner table (Affected Components is a bare list), no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Team assignments are inferred: `RHOAI Serving` for all epics, taken from the KServe architecture-context file (the only team data available). Platform Operator ownership is unverified.\n75\t2. **Platform Operator architecture context absent**: no file in `.context/architecture-context/` covers Platform Operator, so its architecture claims cannot be corroborated (signal 9 scored 0 for E002). If the operator is in fact not in the platform, this decomposition under-scopes provisioning work.\n76\t3. **Serving data plane scope ambiguity**: \"serving data plane\" images are tracked under KServe per the strategy's component list, but may involve Serving Runtimes-owned images. If so, a separate epic (component `Serving Runtimes`) should be spun out at execution time.\n77\t4. **Docs epic priority**: E005 carries P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0) even though its HLR-4 is P2. This is rule-mandated, does not propagate upstream (E004 correctly remains P1), and is noted here because it reads as an inversion against the HLR table.\n78\t5. **Possible priority inversion (HLR-3)**: the CI regression gate is P1 while it protects P0 compliance — a reintroduced non-validated-crypto regression could invalidate certification evidence already submitted. The strategy's P1 label is preserved, but reviewers may want to raise it.\n79\t6. **External schedule risk (not an AC)**: certification lab turnaround is historically six to nine months and review-board cadence is externally controlled; neither can be compressed by engineering effort. Recorded as Risks/Notes on E003, with ACs written at the engineering completion point (package assembled, submitted, triaged).\n80\t\n81\t## Tiered Delivery\n82\t\n83\tThe external certification process has uncertain timing; partial value does not depend on it:\n84\t\n85\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: E001, E002, E004 — FIPS-capable serving-stack images that run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters, plus CI regression enforcement. Deliverable and usable regardless of certification outcome timing.\n86\t- **Tier 2 (externally gated)**: E003 — evidence package submission through the security review board to the accredited lab; the submission is engineering-controlled but the certification outcome and its timing are not. E005 (documentation) follows the evidence work per the docs-last rule.\n87\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac13f1b5084b4195","agent_id":"ad4a5cc8c8142fa9f","session_id":"b1b2271b-4c69-47a7-906c-4aa2a3507a54","uuid":"70e1b41a-0389-4f08-854b-185e64e7294d","timestamp":"2026-10-23T15:54:52.115Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8aaf4faba2ff92e6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9013"}
